{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>To the entire congregation, etc.</b> The reason for His saying ״אל כל״ — our Sages of blessed memory said in Torat Kohanim: because most of the principal subjects of the Torah depend upon this parashah, it was stated in a public assembly. And although for every single commandment among the commandments of the Torah Moses would say it to all Israel, as stated in the baraita brought in Eruvin 54b, the Rav Rabbi Elijah Mizrachi explained that they would enter group after group, whereas in this parashah they all entered together — end of his words. But it is difficult according to his words: if his intent in saying that they entered group after group concerning Israel who came after the elders, if so Moses would have had to speak more than five or six times, and according to the count of times that the Tanna enumerated there, that each one had four, it does not come out so, for they wrote, and this is their language: ״all the people entered,״ etc. ״they were found in Aaron's hand four.״ And if the Rav's intent in saying ״group after group״ is concerning the group of Aaron, and Nadab and Abihu, and the group of the elders etc. — that in other commandments they would enter one after the other, whereas here Aaron, Nadab and Abihu and the elders and the Israelites entered together — then when we stand on the core of the matter: why did Moses conduct himself with all the commandments in this order, and not teach them all together four times, so that each Israelite would have it four times from the mouth of Moses, which is better than hearing the four times only once from Moses and two or three times from his subordinates? Rather, the reason is for one of two things: either to confer honor according to the rank of Israel — honor to Aaron, honor to his sons, honor to the elders, and afterward to Israel; or for the consideration that each one studied on his own from the mouth of Moses, so that when Aaron would review it to his sons and when his sons would review it to the elders and the elders to Israel, through this mediation it would be discernible and perceptible whether there was any change in what was heard between the groups when they reviewed the matter one to another — which would not have been perceived if they had all heard the four times from the mouth of Moses. And this matter will be grasped by the heart of the hearer. If so, for both approaches it would be more proper to act in this order also for this commandment — whether for the reason of conferring honor upon Aaron etc., why should Aaron's honor etc. be diminished here; or for the reason of ensuring the matters were assimilated by the hearers — on the contrary, here this would be even more necessary since they are the principal subjects of the Torah. And if it is for the consideration that Moses would state the commandment in a general way to all Israel — behold, even in the first arrangement there is this consideration, that there is a general address by Moses to all Israel when all Israel enter and Moses reviews it to them in the presence also of Aaron and his sons and the elders; and if so, what is the flaw in the usual order that HaShem commanded to review — is this not merely a diminution?" "<b>Indeed,</b> what is correct in my eyes is this: in the first arrangement Moses would speak only to the men, whereas here HaShem commanded that he speak to the entire congregation of Israel — men, women, and children — in the same order as they were assembled at the giving of the Torah. And further on the verse will be explained in another manner:" "<b>And you shall say to them.</b> The reason for the repetition of saying ״ואמרת״ — to their words of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 24; Torat Kohanim), who said that since most of the principal subjects of the Torah etc. depend on it, therefore He acted regarding it also in the manner of the giving of the Torah, about which it speaks both harshly and gently (Exodus 19:3): ״כה תאמר etc. ותגד״ — and <b>דבר</b> is a term of harshness, <b>ואמרת</b> is gentle speech. And further on the verse will be explained in another manner." "<b>קדשים תהיו (You shall be holy).</b> One needs to know what is the intent of this commandment. And it appears that the verse comes to establish a positive commandment regarding the forbidden relations, concerning which He commanded them in the preceding parashah by way of prohibition — He added a positive commandment upon them, so that one also transgresses them by way of a positive commandment." "Further, it intends according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Kiddushin 39b): if a person sat and did not commit a transgression, he is given reward as one who performs a commandment — and this is the meaning of the verse ״קדשים תהיו״ as a positive commandment: when a transgression comes to one's hand and he distances himself from doing it, thereby he fulfills the positive commandment they were commanded in the statement <b>קדשים תהיו</b>. And He expressed the remembrance of this commandment in this language of <b>קדשים</b>, to say that whoever fulfills this commandment is called holy — and this too is the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 24; Yerushalmi Yevamot 2:4). And for this reason He was precise in saying <b>אל כל עדת בני ישראל</b>, to say that this commandment with which He charges them, ״קדשים תהיו,״ is a commandment that devolves upon every person, for every Israelite who fulfills this commandment is called holy, and there is no rank within Israel that would be excluded from this attainment. Through this, too, the reason for the doubling of ״דבר ואמרת״ will be settled, for there is here a harsh speech with respect to the transgressor, in that a positive commandment is added as punishment, and there is here a virtue and honor for the one who fulfills it, in that he is called holy. Or perhaps it is specifically the children of Israel who possess this quality, unlike the nations of the world — even if a person were to separate himself from aspects of forbidden relations that are prohibited to him, or even from those that are not prohibited, nevertheless he is deprived of the quality of holiness; and therefore He said <b>אל</b> etc. <b>בני ישראל</b>. And further on it will be explained in another manner." "<b>For holy am I, etc.</b> One must know what kind of reason-giving this is — for does the matter logically require the work of His hands to resemble its Maker? Yet there are many levels in the Elohim of Israel that are not attainable in Israel. And if his intent is to give the reason for the commandment — that He desires the thing because our Elohim is holy and desires that His servants also be likewise — that is not the reason that logically follows, and thus far I still do not know why the verse should be required to give a reason." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory, it appears, sensed this difficulty, and they expounded in Torat Kohanim as follows: 'If you sanctify yourselves, I account it upon you as though you sanctized Me' — thus far. According to this, the intent of <b>for holy</b> etc. would be to inform of the benefit that follows, and to inform of its opposite, as the Tanna concluded there: 'And if you do not sanctify yourselves, I account it upon you as though you did not sanctize Me' — thus far. And perhaps it was for this too that He aimed in the doubling of ״speak and say״ — for the difficulty that follows if they do not sanctify themselves, and for the exaltedness of the outcome if they do sanctify themselves:" "<b>Further,</b> it appears to explain the verse in this manner: for inasmuch as HaShem commanded concerning the aspect of desire fixed in human nature — and it is desire that rules over the will — and I have already written in the preceding portion at the verse (Leviticus 18:2) ״כְּמַעֲשֵׂה אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם״ etc., that this aspect man has no power to master except through extreme distancing in the faculty of thought. From this point on, inasmuch as HaShem commanded in its place to fulfill the species [of procreation] and to cleave to a fitting counterpart for a man, there is an opening for evil from this aspect, to be overpowered by it — the aspect of passion that overpowers and defeats the will by turning it — and no man masters himself to guard against the forbidden relations that HaShem commanded concerning them. If so, whichever way you turn: if he performs the commandment 'in the way of a man' (Proverbs 30:19) to fulfill his species — he is stripped of guarding against forbidden relations; if he restrains himself from this species entirely — he nullifies the commandment of procreation and the commandment of yibbum." "Therefore the utterance of HaShem comes here and commands with the pleasantness of holy words <b>you shall be holy</b> — meaning, that they should sanctify themselves in the acts upon which the commandment to perform them has come, meaning that he should not do the thing for the sake of physical desire and to satisfy the appetite that demands to do the thing, but rather he should do the thing in holiness and in purity, just as he wraps himself in tzitzit and dons tefillin on his arm, and he should despise in his mind the appetite that comes from himself — and this is their statement of blessed memory (Nedarim 20b) that 'it should be as though a demon forced him,' and of such a man it may be said that he is holy." "And inasmuch as a person may say: how can we deny what is felt — that the appetite recognizes itself and imparts the flavor of the thing, and there is no power in the faculty of thought to prevent the sensation in this, for its sensation is great and arouses all the senses and the feelings, and overpowers the thought to join with them in tasting the thing — and if so we have returned to the original concern — for this He said <b>for holy am I, YHWH your Elohim</b>, meaning, after the manner of their statement of blessed memory (Tikkunei Zohar 70) that whoever performs one commandment, the name YHWH rests upon the limb with which he performed the commandment, for the word מצוה has within it a hinted name: מ״צ in א״ט־ב״ש [at-bash permutation] = י״ה, and the letters ו״ה — here is the name YHWH; and I have already written in other places (Leviticus 18:4) the reason that half the name is in א״ט־ב״ש and half is revealed. If so, when a person does the thing for the sake of a commandment, the name YHWH rests upon him — and He is holy — and through this no evil thing cascades from the act. And this is what He says: <b>for holy am I, YHWH your Elohim</b>. He was precise in saying <b>your Elohim</b>, to allude to the clinging of HaShem to the one who sanctifies himself to do the act for the sake of a commandment, after the manner of His saying ״but you who cleave to HaShem your Elohim״. And therefore He said <b>to all the congregation of the children of Israel</b>, to say that every man of Israel is within the purview of this commandment; and He also doubled ״and you shall say״ to allude to this virtue." "<b>Further</b>, it intends in His saying <b>for holy am I</b> etc., meaning, that since there are pronouncements of HaShem, blessed be He, in the Torah that the people of Israel are attached to HaKadosh Baruch Hu, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:4) ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים״ etc., and through His servants the prophets it is written, saying (Jeremiah 13:11) ״כַּאֲשֶׁר יִדְבַּק הָאֵזוֹר אֶל מָתְנֵי אִישׁ כֵּן הִדְבַּקְתִּי אֵלַי אֶת כָּל בֵּית יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — from this HaKadosh Baruch Hu comes with an unanswerable claim and says <b>קְדֹשִׁים תִּהְיוּ</b>, and the reason is <b>for holy am I</b>. And if you say, what does straw have to do with wheat (i.e., how can man compare to HaShem?) — for this He said <b>HaShem your Elohim</b>: here He alluded to the indwelling of the Shechinah in attachment to HaShem, as stated in the verse ״וְאַתֶּם הַדְּבֵקִים בַּה׳ אֱלֹהֵיכֶם״." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that a person should not say that the verse prohibited only the act of forbidden relations, but not the dimension of thought and contemplation and the other refinements of lust which are many — and our Sages, of blessed memory, already alluded to some of them (Berakhot 61a), such as one who passes coins from his hand to the hand of a woman etc., as they expounded on the verse ״יָד לְיָד״ etc. (Proverbs 11:21). And here HaShem commanded concerning those things and their like, that one must distance himself from the impurity and from what resembles it and what resembles its resemblance, so as not to come to stumble even in those refinements. As the Zohar states on the verse ״אַל תִּפְנוּ אֶל הָאֱלִילִים״ — and this is what He commanded in the pronouncement ״קְדֹשִׁים תִּהְיוּ״, lest one come to thought and the dimension of holiness be withdrawn from him, as Scripture says (Deuteronomy 23:11) ״לֹא יִהְיֶה טָהוֹר מִקְּרֵה לָיְלָה״. And since a person may say, this matter is not within my control, for even if I guard myself by day, nevertheless at night the matter is not dependent on me — for this He said <b>for holy am I HaShem your Elohim</b>: since I am holy and I am with you, I will hold back the dimension of impurity from approaching the Israelite man, unless a person thinks evil thoughts — then the indwelling of the Shechinah is withdrawn from him, and the dimension of evil rests upon him, and this is the husk that covers the face of a wicked man, and then he will come to a nocturnal emission at night." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along this path: <b>קְדֹשִׁים תִּהְיוּ</b> is in the future tense, meaning there is no cessation to this commandment, for every gate of holiness that one enters, there is still the status of entering yet another gate above it, for there is no measure to the rungs of holiness available to all who wish to take the Name. Go out and learn from the rungs of the prophets — one above the other — and Moses rises above them all; and perhaps there can be a rung greater than Moses, namely the rungs of our king, our Mashiach, who is crowned with crowns of crowns, as is understood from the verse (Isaiah 11:2) ״וְנָחָה עָלָיו רוּחַ ה׳״ etc., and according to the words of our Sages, of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah ch. 19, Deuteronomy Rabbah ch. 2), that Moses himself will be the future redeemer, as we elaborated in our explanation of those matters in their place. If so, there is no measure or boundary to the rungs of holiness. Therefore He said <b>תִּהְיוּ</b>, for this commandment has no cessation, and it always remains within the status of this commandment to be holy." "And He gave the reason for His words <b>for holy am I HaShem your Elohim</b> — that there is no measure to His holiness, blessed be He, and HaShem desires in His sons, His beloved, to resemble their Maker in the supremity of holiness. From this, judge in your own mind the rungs you may attain." "<b>Further</b>, it intends in giving the reason <b>for holy</b> etc., meaning, according to the measure of the greatness of His holiness, blessed be He, we are required to sanctify ourselves. For since we prepare ourselves for the indwelling of the Shechinah upon our heads, we must gauge the magnitude of His holiness, blessed be He, in order to make the necessary preparations for it — for preparing to host an ordinary person in his home is not comparable to preparing to host a great prince, and all the more so a king, and all the more so the son of 'all the more so a king,' and all the more so etc. the King of the universe, to whom belongs sovereignty, such that whatever one does it is still in the category of 'stand and do,' for no measure suffices to require the doing. Therefore He said <b>for holy am I HaShem your Elohim</b>, meaning: according to the measure of My holiness, I command you to prepare yourselves with all achievable capacity." "<b>And you will find</b> that they said, of blessed memory (Shabbat 112a): ״If the earlier ones were like angels, etc., and if the earlier ones were like men, etc.״ — you can see from this how much a person must exert himself in his sanctification. Go out and learn how much the early ones would strive in the service of HaShem, from the incident of Rabbi Elazar ben Azariah (Shabbat 54a), whose neighbor's cow went out with a strap between its horns not in accordance with the will of the Sages, even though according to his own opinion it was permitted, and they said in the Yerushalmi (Shabbat ch. 5) that his teeth were blackened from fasts over that matter. And to this the verse alluded in saying <b>Speak</b> — a term of difficulty, from the perspective of the extraordinary nature of the command. And from the perspective of the apprehensible realm, it says <b>and you shall say</b>. And He said <b>unto all the congregation of the children of Israel</b> lest a person say that this great commandment is not for every individual of Israel, and that the verse spoke only to chosen individuals — therefore He said <b>unto all the congregation of</b> etc., for all of them are included in this commandment. And this is their statement, of blessed memory (Tana Devei Eliyahu; Rambam, Laws of Repentance 5): A person is able to make himself like Moses our Master, peace be upon him." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that <b>you shall be holy</b> as the angels who are called ״holy ones,״ as written (Daniel 8:13) ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֶחָד קָדוֹשׁ,״ and this is along the lines of the verse (Psalms 82:6) ״אֲנִי אָמַרְתִּי אֱלֹהִים אַתֶּם וּבְנֵי עֶלְיוֹן כֻּלְּכֶם.״ And since HaShem, blessed be He, caused His Shekhinah to dwell among the children of Israel and made them the members of His palace in place of the angels — go out and learn from what they said in the Zohar (Zohar vol. II 140a) regarding the time when the Holy One, blessed be He, caused His Shekhinah to dwell among those below: how great was the agitation of the beings on high over the fact that HaShem chose the multitudes of Israel over the multitudes of the angels. And therefore HaShem commanded His chosen multitudes — who are His angels — and they shall be called ״holy ones.״ And since you have entered this category of being multitudes in place of the angels, you must be holy. And He alluded to this detail — that they entered in place of the angelic multitudes — in saying <b>YHWH your Elohim</b>:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {9 "The at-bash permutation of מ״צ yields י״ה: under this letter-substitution cipher (א↔ת, ב↔ש, etc.), מ→י and צ→ה. Together with the remaining letters ו״ה of the word מצוה, these spell the divine name יהוה."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:06.801417641Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}